(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_alternatingcurrent=[{id:"neet-2026-q007",pn:7,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Series LCR resonance",q:"An ac circuit contains a resistance of 1 k\u03A9, a capacitor of 0.1 \u03BCF and an inductor of 1 mH connected in series. The resonance frequency of the circuit is approximately.",o:["10.1 kHz","20.7 kHz","15.9 kHz","13.5 kHz"],c:2,s:`Resonance frequency f\u2080 = 1/(2\u03C0\u221A(LC)).
f\u2080 = 1/(2\u03C0\u221A(1\xD710\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)).
f\u2080 = 15.9 kHz.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q012",pn:12,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Series LCR resonance",q:`An ac voltage V = 220 sin(2 \xD7 10\xB3t) Volt is applied to a series LCR circuit. Then the current amplitude in this circuit is:
(Given : L = 10 mH, C = 25 \u03BCF, R = 100 \u03A9)`,o:["5.5 A","11.0 A","22.0 A","2.2 A"],c:3,s:`Angular frequency of the source \u03C9 = 2 \xD7 10\xB3 rad/s.
Inductive reactance X_L = \u03C9L = (2 \xD7 10\xB3) \xD7 (10 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 20 \u03A9.
Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(\u03C9C) = 1/[(2 \xD7 10\xB3) \xD7 (25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076)] = 20 \u03A9.
X_L = X_C, so the circuit is at resonance and impedance Z = R.
Current amplitude i\u2080 = V\u2080/Z = V\u2080/R = 220/100 = 2.2 A.`},{id:"neet-2026-q033",pn:33,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"AC instantaneous value",q:"The peak value of an alternating current is 5 A and frequency is 60 Hz. How long will the current, starting from zero, take to reach the peak value?",o:["1/240 s","1/30 s","1/120 s","1/60 s"],c:0,s:`Alternating current: i = i_peak sin(\u03C9t).
Here \u03C9 = 2\u03C0f = 2\u03C0 \xD7 60 = 120\u03C0 rad/s.
So i = 5 sin(120\u03C0t).
For the peak value 5 = 5 sin(120\u03C0t), so sin(120\u03C0t) = 1 = sin(\u03C0/2).
Hence 120\u03C0t = \u03C0/2 and t = 1/240 s.`},{id:"neet-2025-q016",pn:16,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Series LCR circuit",q:"To an ac power supply of 220 V at 50 Hz, a resistor of 20 \u03A9, a capacitor of reactance 25 \u03A9 and an inductor of reactance 45 \u03A9 are connected in series. The corresponding current in the circuit and the phase angle between the current and the voltage is, respectively",o:["7.8 A and 30\xB0","7.8 A and 45\xB0","15.6 A and 30\xB0","15.6 A and 45\xB0"],c:1,s:`X_L = 45 \u03A9, X_C = 25 \u03A9, R = 20 \u03A9
I = 220/\u221A((X_L \u2212 X_C)\xB2 + R\xB2) = 220/\u221A((45 \u2212 25)\xB2 + 20\xB2)
I = 220/(2\u221A2) = 11/\u221A2 = 7.779 A
tan \u03C6 = (X_L \u2212 X_C)/R = (45 \u2212 25)/20 = 1
\u03C6 = 45\xB0`},{id:"neet-2024-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Transformer turns ratio",q:"In an ideal transformer, the turns ratio is N\u209A/N\u209B = 1/2. The ratio V\u209B : V\u209A is equal to (the symbols carry their usual meaning) -",o:["1 : 1","1 : 4","1 : 2","2 : 1"],c:3,s:`For a transformer, V\u209B/V\u209A = N\u209B/N\u209A.
N\u209A/N\u209B = 1/2, so N\u209B/N\u209A = 2.
So V\u209B : V\u209A = 2 : 1.`},{id:"neet-2024-q045",pn:45,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Capacitor in AC circuit",q:"A 10 \u03BCF capacitor is connected to a 210 V, 50 Hz source as shown in figure. The peak current in the circuit is nearly (\u03C0 = 3.14):",o:["1.20 A","0.35 A","0.58 A","0.93 A"],c:3,s:`Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(\u03C9C) = 1/(2\u03C0fC) = 1/(2 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 50 \xD7 10 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 1000/3.14 \u03A9.
V_rms = 210 V, so i_rms = V_rms/X_C = 210/X_C.
Peak current = \u221A2 \xD7 i_rms = \u221A2 \xD7 (210/1000) \xD7 3.14 = 0.932 \u2248 0.93 A.`,fig:{w:240,h:205,alt:"A capacitor C = 10 \u03BCF in the top wire of a rectangular circuit, left plate marked plus and right plate minus, and an AC source 210 V, 50 Hz in the bottom wire.",d:[["l",15,28,128,28],["l",143,28,235,28],["l",128,12,128,46],["l",143,12,143,46],["t",124,24,"+","e","s"],["t",124,41,"+","e","s"],["t",148,24,"\u2212","s"],["t",148,41,"\u2212","s"],["t",144,66,"C = 10 \u03BCF","m"],["l",15,28,15,167],["l",235,28,235,167],["l",15,167,87,167],["c",95,167,8],["t",95,170,"~","ms"],["l",103,167,235,167],["t",88,193,"210 V, 50 Hz"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q026",pn:26,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Resonance in LCR circuit",q:"In a series LCR circuit, the inductance L is 10 mH, capacitance C is 1 \xB5F and resistance R is 100 \u03A9. The frequency at which resonance occurs is:",o:["1.59 kHz","15.9 rad/s","15.9 kHz","1.59 rad/s"],c:0,s:`At resonance the frequency is f = 1/(2\u03C0\u221A(LC)).
LC = 10\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 1\xD710\u207B\u2076 = 10\u207B\u2078, so \u221A(LC) = 10\u207B\u2074.
f = 10\u2074/(2\u03C0) = 1.591\xD710\xB3 Hz = 1.591 kHz.`},{id:"neet-2023-q027",pn:27,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Capacitor in ac circuit",q:"An ac source is connected to a capacitor C. Due to decrease in its operating frequency:",o:["capacitive reactance remains constant","capacitive reactance decreases.","displacement current increases.","displacement current decreases."],c:3,s:`Capacitive reactance is X_C = 1/(\u03C9C).
When the frequency falls, \u03C9 falls, so X_C increases.
The current in the circuit therefore decreases.
Conduction current equals displacement current, so the displacement current decreases.`},{id:"neet-2023-q029",pn:29,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Transformer",q:"A 12 V, 60 W lamp is connected to the secondary of a step-down transformer, whose primary is connected to ac mains of 220 V. Assuming the transformer to be ideal, what is the current in the primary winding?",o:["3.7 A","0.27 A","2.7 A","0.37 A"],c:1,s:`For an ideal transformer, input power = output power.
V\xD7I in the primary = 60 W.
220 \xD7 I = 60, so I \u2248 0.27 A.`},{id:"neet-2023-q044",pn:44,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Impedance of LCR circuit",q:"The net impedance of circuit (as shown in figure) will be :",o:["25 \u03A9","10\u221A2 \u03A9","15 \u03A9","5\u221A5 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`L = (50/\u03C0) mH, so X_L = 2\u03C0\xD750\xD7(50/\u03C0)\xD710\u207B\xB3 = 5 \u03A9.
C = (10\xB3/\u03C0) \xB5F, so X_C = 1/(2\u03C0\xD750\xD7(10\xB3/\u03C0)\xD710\u207B\u2076) = 10 \u03A9.
Z = \u221A((X_C \u2212 X_L)\xB2 + R\xB2) = \u221A((10 \u2212 5)\xB2 + 10\xB2) = \u221A125 = 5\u221A5 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:250,h:135,alt:"Series circuit: an inductor of 50/\u03C0 mH, a capacitor of 10\xB3/\u03C0 \xB5F and a 10 \u03A9 resistor along the top, and an ac source (220 V, 50 Hz) at the bottom.",d:[["l",11,20,40,20],["p","M40 20A4 5 0 0 1 48 20A4 5 0 0 1 56 20A4 5 0 0 1 64 20A4 5 0 0 1 72 20"],["l",72,20,105,20],["l",105,11,105,29],["l",111,11,111,29],["l",111,20,160,20],["p","M160 20H163L165.42 16L170.25 24L175.08 16L179.92 24L184.75 16L189.58 24L192 20H195"],["l",195,20,225,20],["l",11,20,11,105],["l",225,20,225,105],["l",11,105,100,105],["l",124,105,225,105],["c",112,105,12,""],["p","M104 105C108 99 110 99 112 105S116 111 120 105"],["t",46,42,"50/\u03C0 mH","m","s"],["t",122,42,"10\xB3/\u03C0 \xB5F","m","s"],["t",185,42,"10\u03A9","m"],["t",135,126,"(220V, 50Hz)"]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q014",pn:14,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"AC through a capacitor",q:`Given below are two statements.
Statement I: In an ac circuit, the current through a capacitor leads the voltage across it.
Statement II: In a.c. circuits containing pure capacitance only, the phase difference between the current and the voltage is \u03C0.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer form the options given below`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are Incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],c:3,s:`In an AC circuit the current through a capacitor leads the voltage across it by a phase \u03C0/2.
So Statement I is correct.
Statement II says \u03C0, which is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2022-q017",pn:17,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"RMS and peak value",q:"The peak voltage of the ac source is equal to",o:["The rms value of the ac source","\u221A2 times the rms value of the ac source","1/\u221A2 times the rms value of the ac source","The value of voltage supplied to the circuit"],c:1,s:`The rms value is E_rms = E\u2080/\u221A2.
So the peak value is E\u2080 = \u221A2 E_rms.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q021",pn:21,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Inductive reactance",q:"An inductor of inductance 2 mH is connected to a 220 V, 50 Hz a.c. source. Let the inductive reactance in the circuit is X\u2081. If a 220 V dc source replaces the ac source in the circuit, then the inductive reactance in the circuit is X\u2082. X\u2081 and X\u2082 respectively are",o:["0.628 \u03A9, infinity","6.25 \u03A9, zero","6.28 \u03A9, infinity","0.628 \u03A9, zero"],c:3,s:`With the AC source: X\u2081 = \u03C9L = 2\u03C0fL = 2\u03C0 \xD7 50 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 0.628 \u03A9.
With a DC source f = 0, so X\u2082 = 2\u03C0fL = 0.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q034",pn:34,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Peak and rms current",q:"A standard filament lamp consumes 100 W when connected to 200 V ac mains supply. The peak current through the bulb will be:",o:["2A","0.707 A","1A","1.414"],c:1,s:`P = 100 W and V_rms = 200 V.
I_rms = P/V_rms = 100/200 = 1/2 A.
I\u2080 = I_rms \xD7 \u221A2 = 0.707 A.`},{id:"neet-2022-q045",pn:45,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Resonance in a series LCR circuit",q:"A series LCR circuit with inductance 10 H, capacitance 10 \u03BCF, resistance 50 \u03A9 is connected to an ac source of voltage, V = 200 sin(100t) volt. If the resonant frequency of the LCR circuit is \u03BD\u2080 and the frequency of the ac source is \u03BD, then",o:["\u03BD\u2080 = \u03BD = 50/\u03C0 Hz","\u03BD\u2080 = 50/\u03C0 Hz, \u03BD = 50 Hz","\u03BD = 100 Hz, \u03BD\u2080 = 100/\u03C0 Hz","\u03BD\u2080 = \u03BD = 50 Hz"],c:0,s:`At resonance \u03C9L = 1/(\u03C9C).
\u03C9\u2080 = 1/\u221A(LC) = 1/\u221A(10 \xD7 10 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 100 rad/s.
Using \u03C9 = 2\u03C0f, \u03BD\u2080 = 100/(2\u03C0) = 50/\u03C0 Hz.
The source V = 200 sin(100t) also has \u03C9 = 100, so \u03BD = 100/(2\u03C0) = 50/\u03C0 Hz.
So \u03BD\u2080 = \u03BD = 50/\u03C0 Hz.`},{id:"neet-2021-q016",pn:16,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"LCR series circuit",q:"An inductor of inductance L, a capacitor of capacitance C and a resistor of resistance 'R' are connected in series to an ac source of potential difference 'V' volts as shown in figure. Potential difference across L, C and R is 40 V, 10 V and 40 V, respectively. The amplitude of current flowing through LCR series circuit is 10\u221A2 A. The impedance of the circuit is",o:["5 \u03A9","4\u221A2 \u03A9","5/\u221A2 \u03A9","4 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`VL and VC are opposite in phase, so net reactive voltage = 40 \u2013 10 = 30 V.
Source voltage V = \u221A(VR\xB2 + (VL \u2013 VC)\xB2) = \u221A(40\xB2 + 30\xB2) = 50 V (rms).
Irms = I\u2080/\u221A2 = 10\u221A2/\u221A2 = 10 A.
Z = V/I = 50/10 = 5 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:330,h:170,alt:"Series loop with an inductor, a capacitor and a resistor on the top wire and an ac source V at the bottom; voltages 40 V, 10 V and 40 V marked across them",d:[["p","M30 30 H65 C65 18 77 18 77 30 C77 18 89 18 89 30 C89 18 101 18 101 30 C101 18 113 18 113 30 H150"],["l",150,19,150,41],["l",157,19,157,41],["l",157,30,230,30],["p","M230 30 H234 L238 20 L244 40 L250 20 L256 40 L262 20 L266 30 H300"],["l",300,30,300,125],["l",30,30,30,125],["l",30,125,150,125],["l",172,125,300,125],["c",161,125,11],["p","M155 125 C158 119 160 119 161 125 C162 131 164 131 167 125"],["t",161,152,"V","m"],["l",78,86,31,86,"a"],["l",102,86,149,86,"a"],["t",90,90,"40 V","ms"],["l",172,86,158,86,"a"],["l",204,86,214,86,"a"],["t",188,90,"10 V","ms"],["l",258,86,216,86,"a"],["l",286,86,299,86,"a"],["t",272,90,"40 V","ms"]]}},{id:"neet-2021-q045",pn:45,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Transformer",q:"A step down transformer connected to an ac mains supply of 220 V is made to operate at 11 V, 44 W lamp. Ignoring power losses in the transformer, what is the current in the primary circuit?",o:["4 A","0.2 A","0.4 A","2 A"],c:1,s:`No losses: input power = output power = 44 W.
Vp Ip = 44, so Ip = 44/220 = 0.2 A.`},{id:"neet-2021-q047",pn:47,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Resonance and half-power frequencies",q:"A series LCR circuit containing 5.0 H inductor, 80 \u03BCF capacitor and 40 \u03A9 resistor is connected to 230 V variable frequency ac source. The angular frequencies of the source at which power transferred to the circuit is half the power at the resonant angular frequency are likely to be-",o:["42 rad/s and 58 rad/s","25 rad/s and 75 rad/s","50 rad/s and 25 rad/s","46 rad/s and 54 rad/s"],c:3,s:`Resonant frequency: \u03C9\u2080 = 1/\u221A(LC) = 1/\u221A(5 \xD7 80 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 1/\u221A(4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 50 rad/s.
Power is half at the two frequencies where the current is 1/\u221A2 of its peak; for a sharp resonance these are \u03C9\u2080 \xB1 R/(2L).
R/(2L) = 40/(2 \xD7 5) = 4 rad/s.
So \u03C9 = 50 \u2013 4 = 46 rad/s and 50 + 4 = 54 rad/s.`},{id:"neet-2020-q108",pn:108,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"LCR circuit power factor",q:"A series LCR circuit is connected to an voltage source. When L is removed from the circuit, the phase difference between current and voltage is \u03C0/3. If instead C is removed from the circuit, the phase difference is again \u03C0/3 between current and voltage. The power factor of the circuit is :",o:["0.5","1.0","\u20131.0","zero"],c:1,s:`Without L: tan \u03C6 = X_C/R. Without C: tan \u03C6 = X_L/R.
The phase differences are equal (\u03C0/3), so X_C = X_L.
So the circuit is in resonance and Z = R.
Power factor = cos \u03C6 = R/Z = 1.`},{id:"neet-2020-q123",pn:123,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Capacitor in AC circuit",q:"A 40 \u03BCF capacitor is connected to a 200 V, 50 Hz ac supply. The rms value of the current in the circuit is, nearly :",o:["2.05 A","2.5 A","25.1 A","1.7 A"],c:1,s:`Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(\u03C9C), so I = V/X_C = V\u03C9C.
\u03C9 = 2\u03C0 \xD7 50 = 314 rad/s.
I = 200 \xD7 314 \xD7 40 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 2.5 A.`}]})();
